# skulkd — example configuration
#
# Copy this next to your deployment and uncomment what you want to change:
#
#     cp skulkd.env.example skulkd.env
#     docker run --env-file skulkd.env ghcr.io/roeeyn/skulkd:latest
#
# Every line here is commented out, so an untouched copy runs the relay on the
# specification's own defaults (§8). The values shown ARE those defaults.
#
# There are no secrets in this file and there is no line to add one. The relay
# holds no keys and issues no credentials: a room's password is hashed with
# argon2id in the memory of the room process that received it and never reaches
# configuration, a log, or a disk. `Skulkd.ConfigTest` fails if a variable that
# sets something secret-shaped ever appears below.
#
# Spec §7.4 spells this interface as command-line flags. The relay reads
# environment variables until M5 packages a binary that can parse flags — see
# entry #2 in docs/deviations.md. The flag names survive as the variable names.
#
# A value that will not parse STOPS THE BOOT, naming the variable. A relay that
# starts with a bound it could not read is worse than one that refuses to,
# because you would believe the limit was in force.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it listens
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Address and port, as IP:PORT. IPv6 must be bracketed: [::]:4000
#
#   0.0.0.0:4000     every interface — what a container wants, since the
#                    container boundary is what limits reachability
#   127.0.0.1:4000   loopback only — what a relay behind a TLS reverse proxy
#                    wants, so that nothing but the proxy can reach it
#
# A hostname is not accepted: resolving one at boot would make where the relay
# listens depend on DNS.
#SKULKD_BIND=0.0.0.0:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Capacity bounds (§8)
#
# These are capacity limits, not rate limits. §19 omits rate limiting from this
# version by design, so none of these will slow an attacker down — they stop the
# relay consuming unbounded memory while one tries. docs/self-hosting.md has the
# blunt version of that sentence, and you should read it before exposing a relay.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long a room survives with no accepted chat message, in MILLISECONDS.
# Only a stored message refreshes it (§14) — connected-but-silent clients do
# not. The default is §8's 120h: 120 * 60 * 60 * 1000.
#
# Milliseconds rather than §7.4's `120h` duration spelling on purpose: a
# duration syntax is a small language with its own bugs, for a value set once.
#SKULKD_ROOM_TTL_MS=432000000

# How many rooms may exist at once. The 10,001st `room.create` is refused with
# `server_capacity` — after expired rooms are purged, and never by deleting a
# live room to make space (§8).
#SKULKD_MAX_ROOMS=10000

# How many participants may be connected to one room. The 33rd joiner is refused
# with `room_full`.
#SKULKD_MAX_MEMBERS_PER_ROOM=32

# How many messages a room retains for replay to whoever joins next. Enforced by
# EVICTING the oldest, not by refusing the newest (§15): a busy room keeps
# working, it just stops remembering the beginning.
#SKULKD_MAX_HISTORY_MESSAGES=1000

# How many bytes of encoded history one room retains. Default 4 MiB:
# 4 * 1024 * 1024. Evicted the same way — except for a single message larger
# than this whole bound, which is refused with `message_too_large` rather than
# emptying the room to fit it.
#SKULKD_MAX_HISTORY_BYTES=4194304

# How many bytes of encoded history the relay retains across every room.
# Default 512 MiB: 512 * 1024 * 1024. This is the bound that REFUSES rather than
# evicting: past it, chat fails with `server_capacity`, because the alternative
# is one busy room evicting another room's conversation.
#
# The practical ceiling is this divided by the per-room bound — 128 rooms at
# 4 MiB each — so lower the per-room number before raising this one.
#SKULKD_MAX_TOTAL_HISTORY_BYTES=536870912

# How many frames one client may fall behind before the relay disconnects it
# abruptly, with no error frame and no close code (nothing the relay could send
# would arrive — the unread queue IS the problem).
#
# Not one of §8's numbers: §21 requires a bound and leaves the figure to the
# implementation. BEST-EFFORT, and worth knowing as such — the relay can see
# frames waiting in a process mailbox, but not the ones already handed to the
# operating system. It cuts off runaway growth; it is not an exact memory ceiling.
#SKULKD_MAX_MEMBER_BACKLOG=500

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The §8 bounds you will NOT find above, and why they are not knobs
#
# §8's table has eleven rows. SIX of them are variables above; the other five are
# enforced and documented, but there is deliberately no variable for them — which
# is the answer to the question a deployer actually asks, so it is written down
# rather than left as silence.
#
# (Two variables above are not from §8 at all: SKULKD_BIND is §7.4, and
# SKULKD_MAX_MEMBER_BACKLOG is §21, which requires a bound and leaves the number
# to the implementation.)
#
# TWO ARE HALF OF A CROSS-LANGUAGE CONTRACT.
#
#   Maximum plaintext message size   4,096 UTF-8 bytes
#   Maximum WebSocket frame size     16,384 bytes
#
# Both are compile-time constants in Skulkd.Protocol, and the Go client carries
# the same two numbers independently. docs/protocol/corpus/ fails CI if the two
# implementations disagree about any frame, so a relay that raised its message
# limit would start accepting frames every client rejects — and drift out of the
# corpus that proves the two agree. These become configurable when both sides can
# negotiate them, which is a protocol change rather than a setting. Entry #2 in
# docs/deviations.md carries the same note.
#
# THREE ARE PROTOCOL VALIDATION, NOT DEPLOYMENT POLICY.
#
#   Maximum room-ID length                160 ASCII bytes
#   Maximum room-password length          256 UTF-8 bytes
#   Minimum user-entered password length  12 UTF-8 bytes
#
# These describe what a valid frame contains, and both implementations check
# them. Raising the room-ID ceiling on one relay would make a room id that only
# that relay accepts; lowering the minimum length of a user-chosen password would
# weaken the one defence §19 leaves standing, on a relay with no rate limiting.
# See docs/protocol-v0.md, which is where a change to any of them belongs.
